\details{
The article gives 327 lakes and this rebuilt set has 314, of which 239 have some
species. The survey lists \emph{D. longiremis} and not the \emph{D. longispina} of Figure 3a of the
article, which we took to be the same taxon.

The tree has branch lengths in millions of years from TimeTree 5 (Kumar et al. 2022), which
dates nine of the 13 species, mostly from mitochondrial genomes (Xu et al. 2021). Its
topology differs from that of Figure 3a: \emph{D. laevis} and \emph{D. dubia} are the sister of
\emph{D. galeata mendotae} and \emph{D. rosea} rather than of all other \emph{Daphnia}. The four species
without dates were placed as in Figure 3a, at arbitrary ages: \emph{D. catawba} and
\emph{D. parvula} split the branch of \emph{D. retrocurva} into thirds, \emph{D. rosea} joins
\emph{D. galeata mendotae} halfway along its branch, and \emph{S. serrulatus} joins \emph{S. vetulus} at
the crown age of \emph{Simocephalus} in TimeTree. The absolute ages are uncertain: for the
ancestor of the \emph{D. longispina} and \emph{D. pulex} groups, Cornetti et al. (2019) found
105 Ma with mitochondrial genes and 31.5 Ma with nuclear genes, so relative ages are more
reliable than absolute ones. The article does not give the area of each lake, so rivers
in \code{biogeography} are approximated by longitude and these contrasts differ from the
original ones: S1, east and west of the Hudson; S2, Adirondacks and other lakes to the
west of the Hudson; S3, east and west of the Connecticut River, east of the Hudson; S4,
Maine and other lakes east of the Hudson; S5, New Hampshire and lakes to its west but
east of the Hudson; S6, New Hampshire and lakes in Rhode Island and Massachusetts.
}
